WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 129: James K. Polk and the Oregon Boundary: Fifty-Four Forty or Fight

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.520
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:06.720
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:11.600
[Rebecca] Today we step into the presidency of America's 11th chief executive, James K.

4
00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:12.480
[Rebecca] Polk.

5
00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:17.760
[Rebecca] Polk is often remembered as the most effective single-term president in American history.

6
00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:25.040
[Rebecca] He promised to accomplish four specific, colossal goals, accomplished all four, and stepped down after four years.

7
00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:29.680
[Rebecca] Today, we look at his battle of wits against the world's greatest empire.

8
00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:40.480
[Rebecca] In 1846, with war with Mexico looming, Polk faced down the British Empire over the disputed Oregon wilderness, securing the American Pacific Northwest.

9
00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:46.800
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story of 5440 or fight.

10
00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:54.960
[George] In May 1844, delegates to the Democratic National Convention gathered in Baltimore in a state of paralyzed deadlock.

11
00:00:54.960 --> 00:01:10.000
[George] The frontrunner, former President Martin Van Buren, held a majority of delegates, but Southern delegates, enraged by Van Buren's opposition to annexing Texas, invoked a rule requiring a two-thirds majority to win the nomination.

12
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:14.560
[George] For eight exhausting ballots, candidates clashed without resolution.

13
00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:21.280
[George] Then, on the ninth ballot, delegates introduced an unexpected name, James Knox Polk of Tennessee.

14
00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:27.120
[George] At age 48, Polk was the first Dark Horse presidential nominee in American history.

15
00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:35.360
[George] A protege of Andrew Jackson, affectionately dubbed Young Hickory, Polk was a former Speaker of the House and Governor of Tennessee.

16
00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:44.160
[George] He was thin, frail, possessed of cold, unblinking gray eyes, and wore his graying hair long, combed straight back behind his ears.

17
00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:46.960
[George] Polk was neither charming nor charismatic.

18
00:01:46.960 --> 00:02:00.520
[George] He was an austere, relentless Presbyterian workhorse who worked 12 to 14 hours every day, never took a single vacation day in his life, and believed in accomplishing his goals through sheer, unbreakable determination.

19
00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:05.000
[George] Polk accepted the nomination with an extraordinary, unprecedented pledge.

20
00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:12.360
[George] He announced that he would serve only a single four-year term, and set out four specific, monumental objectives.

21
00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:22.920
[George] He promised to re-establish the independent treasury, reduce tariffs, acquire California from Mexico, and settle the Oregon boundary dispute with Great Britain.

22
00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:36.120
[George] In November 1844, campaigning on a platform of unapologetic continental expansion, Polk narrowly defeated Whig giant Henry Clay, capturing the presidency on the wings of manifest destiny.

23
00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:40.920
[George] The Oregon Territory was a vast, breathtaking geographic paradise.

24
00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:49.000
[George] Stretching from the 42nd parallel, the northern border of Mexican California, all the way to latitude 54 degrees.

25
00:02:49.000 --> 00:03:00.920
[George] Forty minutes, the southern boundary of Russian Alaska, the region encompassed modern-day Oregon, Washington, Idaho, and portions of Montana, Wyoming, and British Columbia.

26
00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:10.280
[George] It was a wonderland of towering Douglas fir forests, rushing salmon streams, snowcapped volcanic peaks, and the mighty Columbia River.

27
00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:21.320
[George] For nearly three decades, this vast empire had been governed under an extraordinary treaty of joint occupancy, signed in 1818 between the United States and Great Britain.

28
00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:29.080
[George] Under the treaty, citizens of both nations were permitted free access to settle, trade, and fish throughout the territory.

29
00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:35.800
[George] In practice, however, the British had dominated the region through the commercial monopoly of the Hudson's Bay Company.

30
00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:49.680
[George] Operating out of Fort Vancouver on the Columbia River, the legendary chief factor John McLaughlin ruled over an empire of fur trappers, native trade networks and trading posts, keeping American settlements at bay.

31
00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:57.840
[George] For years, previous American presidents had offered to settle the dispute by extending the 49th parallel, the existing U.S.

32
00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:06.880
[George] Canada border east of the Rockies, all the way to the Pacific Ocean, allowing Britain to retain Vancouver Island and the navigation of the Columbia River.

33
00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:20.960
[George] But the British government had repeatedly rejected the offer, insisting that the Columbia River must form the international boundary, which would have given Great Britain all of Western Washington, including the deepwater harbor of Puget Sound.

34
00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:28.880
[George] By the early 1840s, the geopolitical balance in the Pacific Northwest was transformed by a grassroots human migration.

35
00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:31.120
[George] It was called Oregon Fever.

36
00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:35.360
[George] Lured by reports of fertile, mild farmland in the Willamette Valley.

37
00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:47.200
[George] Thousands of American pioneer families packed their belongings into covered Conestoga wagons and set out from Independence, Missouri, on the harrowing 2,000-mile trek along the Oregon Trail.

38
00:04:47.200 --> 00:05:01.840
[George] Surviving cholera outbreaks, flooded river crossings, and the terrifying mountain passes of the Blue Mountains and Cascades, over 5,000 American settlers poured into the Willamette Valley between 1841 and 1844.

39
00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:07.040
[George] The American settlers outnumbered British fur trappers by more than five to one.

40
00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:20.560
[George] In 1843, meeting at Champog, the American pioneers organized their own provisional government, adopted the laws of Iowa, and demanded that the United States extend federal sovereignty over the territory.

41
00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:27.440
[George] In Congress and across Western newspapers, fiery Democratic expansionists championed total victory.

42
00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:33.720
[George] Pointing to the Russian border at the 54th Parallel, they coined the bellicose, thunderous battle cry.

43
00:05:34.040 --> 00:05:48.600
[George] 54-40 or fight, expansionists declared that the entire Oregon Territory was America's by divine providence, and threatened war against Great Britain if the British refused to surrender every square inch of the Pacific Northwest.

44
00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:49.640
[George] When James K.

45
00:05:49.640 --> 00:05:56.040
[George] Polk assumed the presidency in March 1845, he took a firm, unyielding tone.

46
00:05:56.040 --> 00:06:08.920
[George] In his inaugural address, Polk quoted the Democratic Platform, declaring that America's title to the whole of Oregon was clear and unquestionable, and that it was his duty to protect American settlers moving west.

47
00:06:08.920 --> 00:06:12.840
[George] Across the Atlantic, the British press erupted in fury.

48
00:06:12.840 --> 00:06:27.480
[George] The Times of London warned that American arrogance would be met with Royal Navy broadsides, and British Prime Minister Sir Robert Peel announced in Parliament that Britain possessed clear rights in Oregon and was fully prepared to defend them by force of arms.

49
00:06:27.480 --> 00:06:34.440
[George] Yet behind closed doors, Polk was a consummate, calculated realist who understood the limits of bluster.

50
00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:55.480
[George] In July 1845, to test British diplomatic intentions and show reasonable moderation, Polk instructed Secretary of State James Buchanan to quietly renew the traditional American compromise, extending the international boundary along the 49th parallel to the Pacific Ocean, surrendering Vancouver Island to Britain.

51
00:06:55.480 --> 00:06:59.320
[George] But retaining the strategic deepwater ports of Puget Sound.

52
00:06:59.320 --> 00:07:15.040
[George] To Polk's immense annoyance, the arrogant British minister in Washington, Richard Pakenham, summarily rejected the offer out of hand without even transmitting it to the foreign office in London, insolently demanding that America offer far greater concessions.

53
00:07:14.840 --> 00:07:20.080
[George] Polk decided to teach the British Empire an unforgettable lesson in power politics.

54
00:07:20.320 --> 00:07:28.240
[George] Rebuffed by Pakenam, Polk withdrew the compromise offer entirely, refusing to allow Buchanan to negotiate further.

55
00:07:28.240 --> 00:07:38.000
[George] When Buchanan panicked, warning that withdrawing the compromise would cause immediate war with Great Britain, Polk recorded his stubborn determination in his diary.

56
00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:41.680
[George] The only way to treat John Bull is to look him straight in the eye.

57
00:07:41.680 --> 00:07:49.200
[George] If you take a bold stand, he will yield, but if you show weakness or hesitation, he will bully and crush you.

58
00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:55.600
[George] Polk decided to throw the entire crisis into high gear and stare down the greatest naval power on earth.

59
00:07:55.600 --> 00:08:03.920
[George] In December 1845, in his first annual message to Congress, President Polk dropped a geopolitical bombshell.

60
00:08:03.920 --> 00:08:13.040
[George] He requested Congress to authorize him to give Great Britain formal twelve-month notice of the termination of the joint occupancy treaty of 1818.

61
00:08:13.040 --> 00:08:22.480
[George] Once the notice expired, British rights in Oregon would cease, leaving the two nations facing immediate military conflict unless a treaty was signed.

62
00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:33.760
[George] Furthermore, Polk invoked the Monroe Doctrine, reaffirming that European powers would not be permitted to establish new colonies or balance of power systems on the North American continent.

63
00:08:33.760 --> 00:08:42.160
[George] In April 1846, Congress passed the resolution authorizing termination of joint occupancy by overwhelming majorities.

64
00:08:42.160 --> 00:08:44.160
[George] The risk was breathtaking.

65
00:08:44.160 --> 00:08:52.160
[George] By April 1846, relations between the United States and Mexico had collapsed following the annexation of Texas.

66
00:08:52.160 --> 00:09:02.520
[George] General Zachary Taylor had moved American troops to the Rio Grande, and border skirmishes had already erupted, plunging the United States into the Mexican-American War.

67
00:09:02.520 --> 00:09:14.440
[George] If Great Britain refused to compromise, the United States faced the existential nightmare of a two-front war against both Mexico and the world's greatest naval superpower, the British Empire.

68
00:09:14.440 --> 00:09:19.320
[George] James Buchanan and cabinet members panicked, begging Polk to back down.

69
00:09:19.320 --> 00:09:24.440
[George] Polk looked Buchanan in the eye and refused, I will not flinch one inch.

70
00:09:24.440 --> 00:09:26.680
[George] Polk's steely nerve worked.

71
00:09:26.680 --> 00:09:33.400
[George] Across the Atlantic, the British government was facing its own severe domestic and geopolitical crises.

72
00:09:33.400 --> 00:09:44.440
[George] The British Prime Minister, Sir Robert Peel, was locked in a titanic domestic battle to repeal the corn laws to avert catastrophic starvation in Ireland from the potato famine.

73
00:09:44.440 --> 00:09:54.360
[George] Britain could not afford a costly foreign war with its largest trading partner, the United States, which supplied the food and cotton essential to British industry.

74
00:09:54.360 --> 00:10:12.840
[George] Furthermore, the Hudson's Bay Company had quietly informed the British cabinet that the fur trade in the Columbia Valley was exhausted, and that thousands of heavily armed American pioneers in Oregon were impossible to dislodge without sending tens of thousands of British regular troops across the globe.

75
00:10:12.840 --> 00:10:21.240
[George] British Foreign Secretary Lord Aberdeen reprimanded his minister Pakenam for rejecting Polk's compromise offer and drafted a new treaty.

76
00:10:21.240 --> 00:10:29.240
[George] On June 6, 1846, a British naval vessel arrived in Washington carrying the draft treaty prepared by London.

77
00:10:29.240 --> 00:10:33.080
[George] The British conceded virtually everything Polk had demanded.

78
00:10:33.080 --> 00:10:49.200
[George] The international boundary would be extended along the 49th parallel from the Rocky Mountains to the Strait of Georgia, giving the United States the entire Pacific Northwest, including the Puget Sound and the Columbia River Valley, while Britain retained Vancouver Island.

79
00:10:49.200 --> 00:10:59.680
[George] Polk, knowing that Western 5440 or fight radicals would complain that he had compromised, cleverly submitted the treaty to the Senate for prior advice before signing.

80
00:10:59.680 --> 00:11:05.280
[George] On June 12, the Senate approved the treaty by a decisive vote of 38 to 12.

81
00:11:05.280 --> 00:11:10.560
[George] The 30-year Oregon boundary crisis was peacefully, permanently resolved.

82
00:11:10.560 --> 00:11:15.440
[George] The resolution of the Oregon boundary dispute was a diplomatic masterpiece.

83
00:11:15.440 --> 00:11:18.000
[George] In a single stroke, James K.

84
00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:30.960
[George] Polk secured nearly 300,000 square miles of rich, magnificent territory, land that would become the states of Oregon, Washington, and Idaho, as well as parts of Montana and Wyoming.

85
00:11:30.960 --> 00:11:40.480
[George] He secured deep water ports opening American trade to Asia and the Pacific, outmaneuvered the British Empire, and avoided a ruinous two-front war.

86
00:11:40.480 --> 00:11:45.040
[George] With Oregon safely secured, Polk turned his full attention south.

87
00:11:45.040 --> 00:11:58.800
[George] Over the next two years, American armies under Zachary Taylor and Winfield Scott captured Monterey, Buena Vista, Veracruz, and Mexico City, concluding the Treaty of Guadalupe Hidalgo in 1848.

88
00:11:58.800 --> 00:12:10.240
[George] The treaty added California, Nevada, Utah, Arizona, and New Mexico to the American Union, expanding the United States by over 1.2 million square miles.

89
00:12:10.240 --> 00:12:12.480
[George] In four short years, James K.

90
00:12:12.480 --> 00:12:16.080
[George] Polk fulfilled every promise he made to the American people.

91
00:12:16.080 --> 00:12:27.040
[George] He expanded the territory of the United States by more than 60%, transforming the Republic into a continental empire stretching unbroken from the Atlantic to the Pacific Ocean.

92
00:12:27.040 --> 00:12:32.040
[George] Having completed his four monumental goals, Polk kept his final promise.

93
00:12:32.040 --> 00:12:37.320
[George] He refused to seek reelection and retired to Tennessee in March 1849.

94
00:12:37.320 --> 00:12:41.080
[George] But the crushing workload of the presidency had shattered his health.

95
00:12:41.080 --> 00:12:47.320
[George] Just 103 days after leaving office, at age 53, Polk died of cholera.

96
00:12:47.320 --> 00:12:57.400
[George] He gave his life to the presidency, proving that an unyielding will, disciplined vision, and calculated courage could carve an empire of liberty across a continent.

97
00:12:58.040 --> 00:12:58.760
[Rebecca] James K.

98
00:12:58.760 --> 00:13:07.560
[Rebecca] Polk's resolution of the Oregon boundary dispute secured America's Pacific destiny and established the northern border that stands peacefully to this day.

99
00:13:07.560 --> 00:13:10.040
[Rebecca] Next week, we continue with James K.

100
00:13:10.040 --> 00:13:21.240
[Rebecca] Polk's second colossal chapter the Mexican American War, the Conquest of California, and the dramatic discovery of gold at Sutter's Mill that ignited the California Gold Rush.

101
00:13:21.240 --> 00:13:25.880
[Rebecca] Until then, thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

